      "problem": "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve $r=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ?$",
      "worked_solution": "The polar equation $r=2a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ defines a circle of radius $a$ centered at the point $(a,0).$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ and therefore\n\n$$\na=\\frac{(-5)}{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $r=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ gives a circle of radius $|-2.5|=2.5$ centered at the point $(-2.5,0),$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14225098/assets/02093df668e46e73e5204c8d971d06eb99a2e8ee8d33666573a8bea4b62e890b.png)",
